course: history
lesson: 83
title: Toad's Edwardian England: early motor cars, caravans, steam trains, wash day and gold sovereigns
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «The Wind in the Willows» came out in 1908, when King Edward VII was on the throne, so that time is called the Edwardian age. Motor cars were new, noisy and rare, most people still travelled by horse, on foot, by bicycle or by steam train, and washing clothes took a whole day.
explain: Toad's motor-car craze was a real craze of rich people at the time. Cars had no roof and no windscreen, and roads were dusty, so drivers wore goggles, gauntlets, caps and long coats, and the car's horn really went «poop-poop» when you squeezed its rubber bulb.
explain: Before the car, Toad tries a horse-drawn caravan holiday, which was also a new fashion: the first caravan club in Britain was started the year before the book came out.
explain: Toad escapes from prison on a steam engine, dressed as a washerwoman, with no money in his pocket, and pays a gipsy in shillings for breakfast. Each of those things tells us how people lived then.
explain: When you read an old book, look for the old world inside it: it is like a time machine with jokes.
fact: The Edwardian age is the reign of King Edward VII, from 1901 to 1910; «The Wind in the Willows» by Kenneth Grahame was published in 1908.
fact: In 1908 only quite rich people owned motor cars; most people walked, cycled, rode in horse-drawn carts and buses or took the train.
fact: Early cars had open tops and roads were mostly dust and stones, so motorists wore goggles, caps, gauntlets (long gloves) and long dust coats, just like Toad's «motor-clothes».
fact: Many early cars were started by turning a starting handle at the front; it could kick back and hurt the driver's arm, and electric starters only came in from about 1912.
fact: From 1904 a new law in Britain set a speed limit of 20 miles an hour, made every car carry a number plate and made drivers get a licence, though there was no driving test until 1935.
fact: Rich car owners often paid a chauffeur to drive and look after the car; the word comes from French and first meant a man who stoked the fire of a steam engine.
fact: Romany families had lived and travelled in painted horse-drawn wagons for many years, and in 1907 the Caravan Club was started in Britain, so caravan holidays for fun, like Toad's, were a new fashion.
fact: The book calls a traveller a «gipsy»; the Romany people came to Britain about 500 years ago, and judging a whole people by an old idea, such as «they all love horse-dealing», is unfair.
fact: A steam engine had a driver and a fireman, who shovelled coal into the firebox, and a guard who waved a flag and blew a whistle to start the train; riding on the engine, as Toad does, was against the rules.
fact: Before washing machines, Monday was usually wash day: clothes were boiled in a big tub called a copper, scrubbed on a washboard, squeezed through a mangle with two rollers and pressed with heavy irons heated on the stove.
fact: Many poorer women earned money as washerwomen, washing other families' clothes by hand; washing machines only became common in British homes in the 1950s and 1960s.
fact: A gold sovereign was a coin worth one pound; a guinea was worth 21 shillings, and the word was still used for prices long after the coin stopped being made; sixpence was half a shilling.
exercise: When did «The Wind in the Willows» come out? | 1908 | 1865 | 1945 | 1808
exercise: Why is that time called the Edwardian age? | King Edward VII was on the throne | Most people in Britain were called Edward | It was the age of the first cars, named after Edward | A famous inventor called Edward built the first car
exercise: Who owned motor cars in 1908? | Mostly quite rich people | Nearly every family in Britain | Only the police and the army | Nobody, cars came later
exercise: Why did early motorists wear goggles and long coats? | Cars were open and the roads were dusty | It was the law for anyone driving any car | Cars were so cold inside they froze the driver's eyes | Goggles helped them see at night
exercise: How were many early cars started? | By turning a handle at the front | By pressing a button with the key | By pushing them downhill every time | By lighting a fire under the seat
exercise: What was the speed limit for cars in Britain from 1904? | 20 miles an hour | 70 miles an hour | 4 miles an hour | There was no speed limit at all
exercise: What did a new car have to carry from 1904? | A number plate | A spare horse | A red flag | A fireman
exercise: When did the driving test begin in Britain? | 1935 | 1904 | 1885 | 1966
exercise: What does a chauffeur do? | Is paid to drive someone's car | Mends the wheels of carts and caravans | Sells motor-cars in a big shop in town | Rides in front with a flag
exercise: What was new about caravan holidays in Toad's time? | Rich people began taking them for fun | Caravans had only just been invented | Caravans were pulled by cars for the first time | They were the only kind of holiday allowed
exercise: Who shovelled coal on a steam engine? | The fireman | The guard | The chauffeur | The porter
exercise: What was a mangle for? | Squeezing water out of wet washing | Ironing shirts flat with hot plates | Boiling dirty sheets in soap | Hanging clothes up to dry
exercise: What was a copper on wash day? | A big tub for boiling the washing | A policeman who checked the clothes | A copper coin to pay the washerwoman | A copper-coloured soap
exercise: When did washing machines become common in British homes? | In the 1950s and 1960s | In the 1700s | Around the year 1908 | Only in the last ten years
exercise: What was a gold sovereign worth? | One pound | One shilling | One penny | One guinea and a half
exercise: How many shillings were in a guinea? | 21 | 20 | 12 | 100
test: «toad drives like 100 mph lol» How fast could a 1908 car legally go on a British road? | 20 miles an hour | 100 miles an hour | 70 miles an hour | There was no limit
test: Toad sits in the road murmuring «Poop-poop!». Where did that sound come from on real early cars? | Squeezing the rubber bulb of the horn | The engine coughing loudly every time it started | The driver shouting through a long speaking tube | A bell fixed on the front bumper
test: «why didnt they just start the car with the key like normal» What is the best answer? | Many early cars had no electric starter, so you turned a handle at the front | Keys had not been invented yet, so nobody could lock anything at all | Cars started by themselves when the warm sun shone on the engine | You had to push every car down a hill to start it
test: At the booking office Toad reaches for his waistcoat pocket and finds nothing. Why? | He had left his coat and money in the cell, and the cotton gown had no pocket | Washerwomen were not allowed by law to carry any money at all on trains | His coins had fallen out through a hole in his waistcoat pocket | A railway porter had picked his pocket while he slept on the bench
test: «my gran's gran did washing all monday, why so long?» What is the best answer? | Everything was boiled, scrubbed, mangled and ironed by hand | She was very slow because she chatted to the neighbours all the time | Clothes had to be washed in the river and beaten with stones | Monday was the only day of the week there was any water
test: A friend says Toad's caravan was the same as a modern caravan towed by a car. What is wrong? | Toad's caravan was pulled by a horse, as cars were still rare | Toad's caravan was pulled along by a steam engine on rails | Toad's caravan had a big electric motor and batteries | Nothing, it was exactly the same, only yellow
test: The book's gipsy is «very fond of horse-dealing». How should we read that today? | As an old idea about a whole people, which is unfair to them | As a true fact about every Romany person who has ever lived in Britain | As a joke about horses, not people | As a rule written in the law
test: The engine-driver lets Toad ride in the cab, though it is «against the Company's regulations». Why might the rule have existed? | The cab was hot, busy and dangerous, with a fire and moving parts | Washerwomen were thought to bring bad luck to the trains they rode | The cab was kept only for the horses that pulled the trucks | Passengers might steal the coal and sell it in the town
test: Toad pays four shillings for a horse at «a shilling a leg». How much is that in old pence? | 48 pence | 4 pence | 12 pence | 40 pence
test: «chauffeur sounds posh, what did it first mean?» What is right? | A man who stoked the fire of a steam engine | A rich lord who owned many cars | A servant who polished the boots | A French word for a racing driver
test: ★★★ A chauffeur drives at 20 miles an hour for 3 hours, then 10 miles an hour for 1 hour because of the dust. Toad says they went 100 miles. How far did they really go? | 70 miles | 100 miles | 60 miles | 80 miles
exam: Identify the period designated as the Edwardian age. | The reign of King Edward VII, from 1901 to 1910 | The reign of Queen Victoria, from 1837 to 1901 | The period after the Second World War, from 1945 | The years of the Norman Conquest of England
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the attire of early motorists. | Goggles and long coats were worn on account of open cars and dusty roads | Such attire was prescribed by law for all persons using the highway | Goggles were worn exclusively at night, when the lamps dazzled the driver | Long coats were required by law to conceal the wheels
exam: Ascertain the provisions introduced for motor cars in Britain from the year 1904. | A speed limit of 20 miles an hour, number plates and driving licences | The abolition of every limit upon speed throughout the kingdom | A compulsory driving test for every motorist, together with a tax upon horses | A man walking in front with a flag
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises wash day before household washing machines? | It occupied most of a day with boiling, scrubbing, mangling and ironing by hand | It occupied ten minutes once a month, as clothes were seldom changed | It was performed only by machines in great factories in the towns | It required no water whatsoever, the clothes being brushed
